Problem-solving in software engineering is never just about writing code — it’s about how you approach complexity.
Most problems can be solved in a dozen or so ways, very few have a single good solution, so there is no always good or always bad answer.
Even if the end-goal is the same, problem-solving is a process, so where you start will influence the solution you choose.

Two modes of thinking I've came across are top-down and bottom-up.
In this post, I’ll break down what each method really means and where it fits best.

